\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Left: optimal SNR for L1 ($\rho _\mathrm {opt}^{\mathrm {L1}}$, light orange), H1 ($\rho _\mathrm {opt}^{\mathrm {H1}}$, orange), and the network ($\rho _\mathrm {opt}^{\mathrm {net}}$, brown). Right: primary source-frame mass $m_1^{\mathrm {src}}$.}}{87}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Left: true luminosity distance $d_L$ (brown) versus effective luminosity distance $d_L^{\mathrm {eff}}$ (orange). Right: image magnification $\mu $.}}{88}{figure.4.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Source-frame component masses $m_1^{\mathrm {src}}$ and $m_2^{\mathrm {src}}$ for the 500 detectable lensed BBH events selected by \textsc {LeR}, colour-coded by network SNR $\rho _\mathrm {net}$. Diagonal dashed lines mark constant total mass $M$ and dotted lines mark constant mass ratio $q$. The population traces the underlying LIGO O3 Power Law + Gaussian model (\S \ref {sec:LER_population}), with most events clustering near $q=1$ and the highest-SNR events concentrated around $M\sim 50$--$70\,M_\odot $.}}{88}{figure.4.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Distribution of time delays $\Delta t$ between the two lensed images for the 500 detectable lensed BBH events, shown on a logarithmic scale. The distribution spans from minutes to months, consistent with galaxy-scale lenses in the geometric optics regime. The Earth's rotation during this interval is the primary mechanism enabling improved sky localization through joint analysis (\S \ref {sec:joint_PE_skyloc}).}}{89}{figure.4.5}%
